From 428504b73dc78c21b92b66f5b4a192867451eb5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Medium1992 <Medium1992@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:42:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 28/28] XHTTP: Bound what one connection's client holds of a
 governed server's data

A stream's cap follows its own reader, but many streams that start at once on
a fast link each look fast for a moment, and the client has to hold their sum:
twenty slow readers on a gigabit link took a client past 40 MiB of anonymous
memory within about a second, governor on or off.

The streams of one server connection now share "h2Flow.maxConnectionSendWindow":
unread data plus unused window over all of them, 4 MiB unless set, -1 for no
limit. A client that itself grants one stream more than that has the room, so
its own stream window is the limit then; a bridge with raised windows is not
held to what suits a phone on the same inbound. A stream always has the
protocol's default window, so none waits for the others.
